        directions = [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]
        m, n = len(maze), len(maze[0])
        visited = set()

        def dfs(x, y):
            if [x, y] == destination:
                return True
            if (x, y) in visited:
                return False
            visited.add((x, y))

            for dx, dy in directions:
                nx, ny = x, y
                # Roll the ball until it hits a wall
                while 0 <= nx + dx < m and 0 <= ny + dy < n and maze[nx + dx][ny + dy] == 0:
                    nx += dx
                    ny += dy
                # Recursively explore the new stopping point
                if dfs(nx, ny):
                    return True
            return False

        return dfs(start[0], start[1])
```

This solution uses Depth-First Search (DFS) to explore all possible paths the ball can take in the maze. The ball rolls in a direction until it hits a wall and then stops. From each stopping point, the ball can roll in any of the four directions. The algorithm uses a set to keep track of visited positions to avoid infinite loops and ensures that the ball stops at the destination.

