class Solution:
    def minimumMoves(self, arr: List[int]) -> int:
        n = len(arr)
        # dp[i][j] will be the minimum number of moves needed to remove the subarray arr[i:j+1]
        dp = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]

        # Base case: single element subarrays
        for i in range(n):
            dp[i][i] = 1

        # Fill the table
        for length in range(2, n + 1):  # length of the subarray
            for i in range(n - length + 1):
                j = i + length - 1
                if length == 2:
                    dp[i][j] = 1 if arr[i] == arr[j] else 2
                else:
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i + 1][j - 1] if arr[i] == arr[j] else float('inf')
                    for k in range(i, j):
                        dp[i][j] = min(dp[i][j], dp[i][k] + dp[k + 1][j])

        return dp[0][n - 1]
```

This solution uses dynamic programming to solve the problem. The `dp` table is used to store the minimum number of moves needed to remove each subarray `arr[i:j+1]`. The base case is when the subarray has only one element, which takes one move to remove. For longer subarrays, the solution checks if the subarray is palindromic and uses previously computed results to fill in the table. The final result is the minimum number of moves needed to remove the entire array, stored in `dp[0][n-1]`.

